 "title": "Why Alex Hormozi Stopped Chasing Virality for Business Growth",
 "topic": "content creation",
 "summary": "Alex Hormozi has shifted away from viral content formats like memes or fancy editing because virality does not guarantee business results. Content strategy should focus on attracting the right audience that actually converts into business. Creators can achieve this by helping people, using authentic formats, and sharing unique perspectives.",
 "key_points": [
  "Prioritize helping people over attempting to hack the social media algorithm.",
  "Use content formats that fit your authentic style rather than copying trending or viral formats.",
  "Focus on sharing unique perspectives, personal experiences, novel ideas, and real proof instead of generic information.",
  "Virality is useless to a business owner unless it directly translates into more business.",
  "Aim to get attention from the right audience who wants to buy rather than accumulating empty views."
 ],
 "content": "Post text:\nTop content creators like @hormozi are making this MASSIVE shift in their content – and it’s highlighting something VERY important about what it really takes to make money from social media…\n\nNow. It’s important to note that most business owners posting on social media WILL necessarily have a different approach to content strategy than someone with a social media following of over ten million people.\n\nObviously, the Hormozis don’t NEED to get multi millions of eyeballs on their content because they already have multi millions of followers.\n\nBut arguably, you don’t need multi millions of eyeballs either. You just need enough of the RIGHT eyeballs that want to buy what you have to sell.\n\nNow this is not to say “viral = bad.” Especially if you’re starting out, going viral (in the right way) and having more top of funnel content can help push you out there TO the right people. \n\nHowever, if you want to use social media to grow your business, I recommend your top of funnel content always be strategic and part of an overall strategy that still prioritizes resonating with the right people – instead of just “what content will get me the most views.”\n\nA perfect example is one of my clients, who only has about 25k followers across platforms and has zero videos above a million views – and yet she had to pay for someone to help manage all the leads flooding her DMs and comments section. \n\nBecause instead of focusing on making her go viral, we focused on these 3 steps, to get her hyper-targeted message in front of the right people in such a way that they are begging to work with her. \n\nI’d prefer that over millions of empty views any day.\n\nMake a note of this post.\n\nTranscript:\nHas anyone noticed that Alex Hormozi, one of the trailblazers of social media for business, is not going viral anymore? Take a look at his content. There are no memes, no trending formats, no fancy editing. Now, why with all the money at his fingertips, would he choose not to go viral? It's because he understands that virality means nothing to a business owner if it does not result in more business. So he's focusing on content that gets him more business. And here's how you're going to do it for your business. First, you're going to focus on helping people rather than hacking the algorithm, because that will always get you the right kind of viewers. Second, you're going to focus on formats that work for you, rather than formats that are going viral. People are looking for content that feels authentic, not just what's a copy of everybody else. And third, you're going to focus on perspective, not information. Because everyone can find the same information, but it's the novel ideas and the personal experiences and the real proof that gets the right people's attention, builds their trust, and motivates them to buy from you.\n\nOn-screen text:\n- Why Alex Hormozi is NOT going viral anymore\n- Virality 👎\n- More business 👍\n- ✅ Helping people\n- ❌ Hacking the algorithm\n- ✅ Formats that fit YOU\n- ❌ Formats that are viral\n- ✅ Perspective\n- ❌ Information",
 "status": "done",
